+### Dynamic Zernike Kernel Computation
+A technique to compute nearfield-to-farfield transformation kernels dynamically on the GPU during propagation, avoiding the memory cost of caching precomputed kernels for large numbers of spots (e.g., $O(10^4)$ spots) [S2, unofficial]. Each GPU parallel thread locally computes the desired Zernike basis for a specific pixel $(x, y)$ of the megapixel nearfield [S2, unofficial]. The dimensionality of the aberration-space vectors is $D$, representing the number of basis modes used to describe the $N$ spot positions [S2, unofficial]. The layout uses an $N \times D$ matrix $w_d$ where each row corresponds to a spot's position in aberration space, encoded as a $D$-dimensional vector [S2, unofficial]. Caching kernel data for $O(10^4)$ spots with 32-bit precision on a megapixel SLM requires ~40 GB of memory, which is prohibitive for commonplace GPUs [S2, unofficial]. Dynamic computation is faster than loading cached data onto the GPU because the basis is computed in register memory and amortized over many farfield spots [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The method adds a per-object-point phase computation cost. Interpolation of coefficients for unmeasured points adds a lookup/interpolation step.
+*   **Complexity:** The method adds a per-object-point phase computation cost. Interpolation of coefficients for unmeasured points adds a lookup/interpolation step [S1].
 *   **Performance:** The correction is applied during hologram generation. The double-phase encoding step is trivial to compute after the complex-valued hologram is obtained [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Offers spatially variant correction with practically unlimited degrees of freedom, akin to using an independently customized complex lens for each point [S1]. Corrects both display hardware aberrations and viewer vision deficiencies [S1].
